@startuml

' Names: every element carries a unit aware alias (see the conventions below), which
' is unique across the whole diagram. "set namespaceSeparator none" switches the
' namespace notation from hierarchical to purely visual grouping, so that an alias
' resolves to the same element everywhere instead of being scoped to the namespace it
' is declared in. Without it, an alias referenced from outside its namespace does not
' resolve and PlantUML silently creates a phantom element of that name.
set namespaceSeparator none

' Layout: the unit clustering makes the default top down layout grow into one very
' wide row of unit boxes with long inter unit edges. Ranking left to right stacks the
' units instead, the tightened separations compensate the height this costs.
left to right direction
skinparam nodesep 15
skinparam ranksep 50

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Modelling conventions for this diagram
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' * This diagram describes the user facing public API of mw::com. It is the
'   reference for the FMEA (see dependability/safety_analysis/failure_modes.trlc).
' * The declaration keyword states what the modelled element is:
'   class     -> instantiation is possible
'   interface -> all operations are pure virtual
'   abstract  -> only partly implemented
'   Every element of this diagram is a concrete, instantiable C++ class (or class
'   template) and is therefore declared as a class. mw::com exposes no pure
'   virtual or partly implemented type to its users: the abstract parts of the
'   implementation (the *Base and *Binding classes) are not user facing and are
'   therefore not modelled here. The only exception is the <<free_functions>>
'   stereotype, which marks a mere grouping of free functions and not a type;
'   it is modelled as a class because UML has no notation for a free function,
'   but it cannot be instantiated.
' * The public API of base classes (ProxyBase, ProxyEventBase, SkeletonBase,
'   SkeletonEventBase, ProxyFieldBase, SkeletonFieldBase) is flattened into the
'   derived user facing class instead of being modelled separately.
' * A class template carries its template parameters in angle brackets after the
'   class name. UML knows no parameter pack, so a C++ pack is modelled as a
'   single parameter name: ArgTypes for the in args of a method. The field tags
'   (WithGetter / WithSetter / WithNotifier) are not modelled as a parameter at
'   all, since a field is modelled once per tag, see below.
'   Parameter names follow the role the type plays in the public API, which is
'   also the name the trait aliases use (SampleType, FieldType); the
'   implementation partly spells them differently (SampleDataType).
'   Proxy and Skeleton are parameterised with the service interface class
'   template written by the user, which in C++ is a template template parameter.
'   The user binds it via "using TheProxy = AsProxy<TheInterface>" resp.
'   AsSkeleton<TheInterface>. AsProxy / AsSkeleton are alias names of the
'   classes modelled here as Proxy / Skeleton and not types of their own.
'   Proxy derives from Interface<ProxyTrait> and Skeleton from
'   Interface<SkeletonTrait>, i.e. from the interface class of the user, which
'   declares the service elements. This is why the service elements are modelled
'   as composed parts of Proxy / Skeleton, following the convention above that
'   the API of a base class is flattened into the user facing class.
' * Overloads are disambiguated by a name suffix (e.g. FindServiceBySpecifier /
'   FindServiceByIdentifier) since UML does not allow duplicate signatures.
'   Invoke / InvokeWithAllocatedArgs denote the two C++ operator() overloads of
'   ProxyMethod.
' * ProxyMethod is modelled once per partial specialization, because the public
'   API differs between them: only the specializations which declare in args
'   offer Allocate(), and the call returns Result<Void> when the method
'   signature has no return type. The primary template is a static_assert and is
'   therefore not modelled. SkeletonMethod needs no such split, since a single
'   specialization covers all method signatures (its primary template is a
'   static_assert as well). It is therefore modelled as a single class, but
'   with one RegisterHandler overload per method signature category, because the
'   callable signature a handler has to provide differs between them. Following
'   the overload convention above, the overloads carry a name suffix:
'   RegisterHandlerWithInArgsAndReturn   -> void(ReturnType&, const ArgTypes&...)
'   RegisterHandlerWithInArgs            -> void(const ArgTypes&...)
'   RegisterHandlerWithReturnType        -> void(ReturnType&)
'   RegisterHandlerWithoutInArgsOrReturn -> void()
' * A field is modelled once per field tag, for the same reason: the public API
'   a field offers depends on the tags it declares. The variant classes carry
'   the name suffix of their tag: ProxyFieldWithNotifier / ProxyFieldWithGetter
'   / ProxyFieldWithSetter and SkeletonFieldWithSetter. A declared field offers
'   the union of the operations of the variants its tags select, hence no
'   generic ProxyField resp. SkeletonField class is modelled.
'   Tags whose public API is identical are merged into a single element whose
'   name names the alternative, because identical operations over an identical
'   implementation have identical failure modes. That is the case for
'   SkeletonFieldWithNotifierOrGetter: on the provider side neither tag gates an
'   operation and publishing is tag independent, the tags only select which
'   dispatch the consumer uses. On the proxy side the same two tags do enable
'   disjoint operations and therefore stay separate.
'   Tag combinations are deliberately not modelled as separate elements, since
'   every combination reuses the same implementation and therefore has the same
'   failure modes as the variants it is composed of. The tags themselves are
'   modelled as <<tag>> classes, because they are public types the user has to
'   name.
' * MethodReturnTypePtr and MethodInArgTypePtr are modelled as two separate
'   classes although both are aliases of the same class template, because
'   the public API exposes two distinct user facing names with distinct roles:
'   the return type pointer is read by the user after a method call, the in arg
'   pointer is written by the user before invoking the method.
' * The group of in arg pointers returned by Allocate() is modelled as its own
'   ordered datatype MethodInArgTypePtrs instead of being expressed inline on
'   the return type, because UML allows a multiplicity only after the return
'   type of an operation and a template binding substitutes a type, never a
'   multiplicity. Nesting the multiplicity inside the Result binding (e.g.
'   Result<MethodInArgTypePtr[1..*]>) is therefore not valid UML. Modelling it
'   as a separate datatype keeps the statement exact: Allocate() returns exactly
'   one Result, which carries exactly one ordered group holding one pointer per
'   method argument. The zero-copy call operator in contrast takes the pointers
'   as separate parameters and is modelled with a parameter multiplicity, which
'   is valid UML.
' * Special members are modelled, since UML has no constructor / destructor /
'   assignment syntax, under a descriptive name, listed first in the class in
'   this order:
'   default constructor           -> ConstructDefault()
'   user declared constructor     -> Construct(...)
'   destructor                    -> Destroy()
'   copy constructor              -> CopyConstruct(other : const T&)
'   copy assignment               -> CopyAssign(other : const T&) : T&
'   move constructor              -> MoveConstruct(other : T&&)
'   move assignment               -> MoveAssign(other : T&&) : T&
'   converting move constructor   -> MoveConstructOther<Name>(...)
'   operator=(std::nullptr_t)     -> AssignNull()
'   The visibility states whether the user may call the member:
'   +  callable: user provided, = default or implicitly generated
'   -  NOT callable: = delete, suppressed (not declared and not generated,
'      e.g. no default constructor because another constructor is declared)
'      or implicitly defined as deleted
'   -  NOT to be called by the user although C++ makes it public: the default
'      constructors of SamplePtr and SampleAllocateePtr. An instance is only
'      obtained from the middleware (GetNewSamples() / Allocate()).
'   "-" therefore does NOT claim the C++ access specifier private. It is used
'   because the tooling accepts no {deleted} property or "= delete" suffix on an
'   operation, and because a "-" constructor next to a {static} +Create() is
'   the UML idiom for "only the factory produces an instance".
'   The base class special members are flattened like all other base class
'   members, so a special member is shown once on the user facing class.
'   Construct() of a service element takes the owning Proxy / Skeleton, which
'   in C++ is a ProxyBase& / SkeletonBase&; the parameter names are the C++
'   ones. Destroy() matters wherever object lifetime has an observable effect
'   (e.g. implicit StopOfferService / Unsubscribe).
' * Class scoped operations carry the {static} modifier, which UML renders
'   underlined. They are invoked on the type, not on an instance: the static
'   member functions of Proxy / Skeleton / GenericProxy / GenericSkeleton
'   (the named constructors and the whole service discovery entry point) and
'   the named constructors of InstanceSpecifier / InstanceIdentifier, plus the
'   free functions of the runtime <<free_functions>> grouping.
'   Not marked, although they are no member functions in C++ either:
'   - CreateFromCommandLine() / CreateFromPath() of RuntimeConfiguration and the
'     Construct*() special members, which are constructors and therefore
'     operate on the instance being created
'   - IsEqual() / LessThan() / Hash(), which are free or friend functions but
'     are modelled, per the operator convention below, as operations of the
'     instance forming the left hand side of the comparison.
' * Every operation is preceded by a commented out line holding the strict UML
'   operation signature, i.e. parameter direction (in / out / inout) and
'   multiplicity for every parameter and for the return value. Operations
'   returning Void have no return parameter in UML and therefore carry no
'   return type. Multiplicity is [1] unless the element is optional ([0..1],
'   e.g. a possibly null pointer) or a variadic argument pack ([0..*]).
'   These lines are commented out because the tooling does not support this
'   syntax yet; once it does, uncomment them and drop the line below each.
' * {query} marks an operation whose execution leaves the state of the system
'   unchanged. UML has no const qualifier, so {query} is the UML notation for a
'   const qualified member function in C++. Because {query} is only valid at the
'   end of the operation signature, which the tooling does not accept, it is
'   written on the commented out strict UML line only, while the line below it
'   carries the C++ const qualifier instead. An operation is marked if and only
'   if it is a const qualified member function. Free and static functions are
'   never marked, even when they are free of side effects. That is why the
'   comparison and hash operations (free resp. friend functions, see above), the
'   runtime operations and the FindService* operations carry no {query}.
'   ProxyEvent::GetSubscriptionState(), ::GetFreeSampleCount() and
'   ::GetNumNewSamplesAvailable() are queries while the identically named
'   operations of ProxyFieldWithNotifier are not, because the latter are not
'   const qualified in the implementation. This is intentionally modelled as is
'   instead of being harmonised.
' * {readOnly} is a property of an attribute or of an association end, not of an
'   operation and not of a return value. It is therefore only used where the
'   referenced data cannot be modified through the element carrying it,
'   currently only the events end of the association between
'   GenericSkeletonServiceElementInfo and EventInfo. Unlike {query} it does not
'   need a commented out strict UML line, because an association end accepts a
'   property string and a multiplicity in the notation the tooling supports.
' * User supplied callables (handlers) are modelled as a class with the
'   <<callback>> stereotype and a single Invoke() operation carrying the
'   signature of the callable, because UML has no function type. Invoke()
'   follows the operator naming convention above, where operator() is modelled
'   as Invoke() too (see ProxyMethod*). Only EventReceiveHandler,
'   SubscriptionStateChangeHandler, FindServiceHandler and
'   ReceiveHandlerRegistrationChangedCallback are types of the public C++ API.
'   SetHandler, SampleReceiver and MethodHandler are not: the implementation
'   accepts any callable there, so the name only denotes the expected signature.
' * Types which are defined outside mw::com (Path, Json, DataTypeSizeInfo) and
'   types whose content is not part of the user facing contract are
'   declared as <<datatype>> without members, so that every operation signature
'   refers to a modelled element, with a note naming their origin.
' * Relationships are modelled because the FMEA needs them to follow how a
'   failure on one interface propagates to another. Notation used:
'   A *-- B                 composition: B is owned by A (service elements of a
'                           Proxy / Skeleton, members held by value). Its
'                           lifetime ends with A.
'   A o-- "n" B             aggregation: A holds n B without owning them, e.g. a
'                           container or a non owning view. Their lifetime is
'                           independent of A.
'   A ..> B <<use>>         B is consumed by A as an operation parameter or is a
'                           precondition for using A. The field tags are drawn
'                           this way, since a variant can only be used when its
'                           tag is declared.
'   {one of}                attached to several dependencies of the same client:
'                           the client requires at least one of the constrained
'                           suppliers and may use several of them. Used for the
'                           two tags of SkeletonFieldWithNotifierOrGetter. UML
'                           dependencies are conjunctive, so the unconstrained
'                           pair would read as requiring both. {xor} is not used,
'                           because it would additionally exclude declaring both
'                           tags together, which is legal.
'   Composition and aggregation carry the role name of the member they stand for
'   and its multiplicity. Attributes whose type is a modelled element are drawn
'   that way instead of being kept as attributes (EventInfo and
'   GenericSkeletonServiceElementInfo), because the edge records whether the
'   owner holds the element by value or only references it, which the attribute
'   notation cannot express, and because it keeps the element reachable for the
'   FMEA instead of leaving it dangling. An attribute whose type is not modelled
'   (e.g. String) stays an attribute.
'   Only relationships between elements modelled in this diagram are drawn. Edges
'   are grouped per section instead of in one block at the end of the file.
'   One kind of reference is deliberately left without an edge: Result, which
'   wraps the return value of every fallible operation. Drawing it would add one
'   element with more than twenty incoming dependencies without telling the FMEA
'   anything, since the dependency is on the outcome of the operation, not on a
'   separate interface. ComErrc / ComErrorDomain are not modelled at all for the
'   same reason: they are abstracted by Result.
' * Conversion, comparison and hash operators are modelled, since UML has no
'   operator syntax, under a descriptive name following the convention used for
'   operator* / operator-> (Dereference / MemberAccess):
'   explicit operator bool()  -> IsValid()
'   operator==                -> IsEqual()
'   operator!=                -> IsNotEqual()
'   operator=(std::nullptr_t) -> AssignNull()
'   operator<                 -> LessThan()
'   operator>                 -> GreaterThan()
'   std::hash specialization  -> Hash()
'   Comparison and hash operators which are free or friend functions in C++ are
'   modelled as an operation of the type they compare / hash, with the left hand
'   side being the instance the operation is invoked on.
'   operator!= is modelled as its own operation, even though it is verified to
'   delegate to operator== (instance_specifier.cpp: every overload is
'   "return !(lhs == rhs);"), so that the modelled interface matches the set of
'   callable C++ entry points one to one and the safety analysis can be walked
'   against it without consulting the implementation. Note that the delegation
'   is a property of the implementation, not of the language: the code base is
'   pre C++20, so operator!= is a separately compiled function rather than a
'   rewritten operator== candidate.
'   operator!= exists only on InstanceSpecifier (three overloads), SamplePtr and
'   SampleAllocateePtr, which is why IsNotEqual appears only there.
'   InstanceIdentifier, HandleType and FindServiceHandle declare only
'   operator== and operator<.
'   The free operator== / operator!= of SamplePtr compare against nullptr, with
'   the nullptr on either side (four overloads); they are modelled as
'   IsEqual(other : std::nullptr_t) / IsNotEqual(other : std::nullptr_t). The
'   free operator== / operator!= of SampleAllocateePtr compare two pointers of
'   possibly different sample types and are modelled with the other sample type
'   as OtherSampleType.
' * An operation may carry an UNTYPED PARAMETER, e.g. IsEqual(other) and
'   IsNotEqual(other), when several C++ overloads are the same semantic
'   operation applied to different input types and the input type is not
'   distinguished at this level of sub
'   division. UML 2.5.1 has no union type (verified: the only normative "union"
'   is the {union} derived union keyword for Property subsetting), and the
'   alternatives do not fit: a template operation (9.6.4) would assert that the
'   C++ functions are templates, which they are not, and a common supertype
'   does not exist for e.g. InstanceSpecifier and String. The untyped parameter
'   is well formed against the metamodel, where TypedElement::type has
'   multiplicity [0..1] (UML 2.5.1, 7.8.22.5). Note that the printed parameter
'   notation grammar in 9.4.4 does not bracket "':' <type-expression>" as
'   optional, unlike the Property notation (9.5.4) and the operation return
'   type (9.6.4); the specification does not reconcile the two, and the
'   metamodel is followed here.
'   {abstract} is deliberately NOT used for this purpose: per UML 2.5.1 9.9.2.5
'   isAbstract means the feature "does not have an implementation, and one must
'   be supplied by a more specific Classifier", with the constraint
'   abstract_no_method "isAbstract implies method->isEmpty()" (9.9.2.8). It says
'   nothing about parameter types, and asserting it would state that the
'   operation is unimplemented, which is false.
'   The note on the class records which C++ overloads the operation covers, so
'   that the enumeration stays complete. If the variants are later found to
'   have different failure modes, the operation is split at that point.
' * SAFETY ANALYSIS SCOPE. The comparison and hash operations of the service
'   instance identifiers, i.e. IsEqual(), IsNotEqual(), LessThan() and Hash()
'   of InstanceSpecifier, InstanceIdentifier, HandleType and FindServiceHandle,
'   are side effect free and confined to immutable, process local state: no
'   error return channel, no shared memory access, no binding interaction, no
'   OS resource and no cross process effect. Walking the FMEA fault models
'   against them yields no plausible failure mode able to propagate to a
'   safety goal, so they are DISMISSED FROM THE SAFETY ANALYSIS as a class,
'   with this one rationale instead of one record per operation. They are
'   addressed by systematic fault avoidance (unit test, static analysis, code
'   review) rather than by a FailureMode.
'   The dismissal is written down so that it is auditable rather than implied
'   by absence: every other operation is in scope and has to be walked method
'   by method, which is what the safety analysis process requires. The note on
'   each of the four classes repeats which of its operations are covered, so
'   that the statement is visible in the rendered diagram and not only here.
'   ToString() is deliberately NOT covered, although it is const and free of
'   side effects, because its result is the serialised form consumed by
'   InstanceIdentifier::Create() and therefore crosses a process or
'   configuration boundary.
'   Reference: IEC 60812:2018, 3.1.7, which defines the element as the "level
'   of sub-division ... at which failure modes are to be identified", i.e. the
'   granularity is a recorded choice.
' * Intentionally NOT shown, because they are not user facing:
'   - mocking / test hooks (InjectMock, IRuntime, RuntimeMockHolder)
'   - internal lifecycle hooks (PrepareOffer, PrepareStopOffer, Deinitialize,
'     SetupMethods, InitializeInArgsAndReturnValues)
'   - MethodReturnTypePtr / MethodInArgTypePtr GetQueuePosition(), which only
'     serves the internal argument consistency check of ProxyMethod
'   - "*View" / "*Attorney" helper classes
'   - the stream operators (operator<<) which only enable logging of a type via
'     mw::log::LogStream / std::ostream
'   - compile time only trait classes (ProxyTrait, SkeletonTrait), which only
'     map the service element types of a Proxy resp. Skeleton
' * The diagram is clustered per architectural unit: every element sits in the
'   namespace of the unit which owns it. The grouping names are the unit names of
'   the static architecture (overall.puml, binding_independent.puml), which are also
'   the unit design folders under score/mw/com/impl/dependability/, written as the
'   grouping path and prefixed with the name of the SEooC which owns the unit. A unit
'   nested in a component carries that component in the path as well, a unit owned
'   directly by the SEooC does not:
'     mw_com::binding_independent::sample_management_pointers  (unit of a component)
'     mw_com::service_instance_identifiers                     (unit of the SEooC)
'   These namespaces denote ARCHITECTURAL UNITS, not C++ namespaces: there is no
'   score::mw::com::mw_com::binding_independent::... in the code. PlantUML offers no
'   other grouping that nests inside a namespace (a package inside a namespace is a
'   parse error), which is why the unit grouping reuses the namespace notation.
'   Elements which no single unit owns are left directly in score::mw::com:
'   - Path / Json / DataTypeSizeInfo, which are defined outside mw::com altogether
' * Every element is declared as <keyword> "<Name>" as <alias>, i.e. the rendered
'   name is the quoted label while the alias is what every relationship, note and
'   cross reference names. The alias spells out which unit owns the element and is
'   therefore unique across the whole diagram. It is the grouping path of the unit
'   with :: replaced by _, followed by the element name:
'     mw_com_binding_independent_<unit>_<Name>  for a unit nested in a component
'     mw_com_<unit>_<Name>                      for a unit owned by the SEooC
'     mw_com_<Name>                             for an element without owning unit
'   The one exception are the elements which model a type that is defined outside
'   mw::com: they are aliased by their real fully qualified type name with :: replaced
'   by _ (score_filesystem_Path, score_json_Any, score_memory_DataTypeSizeInfo), so
'   that a reference names the actual type instead of claiming mw_com owns it. Do not
'   "normalize" these to the mw_com_ prefix.
'   A class template carries its template parameters in the quoted label, not in the
'   alias (e.g. "ProxyEvent<SampleType>"), so that the alias stays a plain
'   identifier. The alias is deliberately redundant with the enclosing namespace: it
'   makes every reference self describing and lets a reference be read without
'   tracking which namespace it is written in.
'   The aliases are only unambiguous because "set namespaceSeparator none" (see the
'   top of the file) turns the namespaces into a purely visual grouping. Without it an
'   alias is scoped to its namespace, and a reference from outside does not resolve
'   but silently creates a phantom element of that name instead of failing. The same
'   silent failure mode applies to a misspelled alias, so a rendered element whose
'   name looks like an alias (mw_com_...) is always a typo, never an element.
' * The diagram is larger than PlantUML renders into a bitmap by default: the
'   PLANTUML_LIMIT_SIZE of 4096 pixels silently CROPS a PNG rendering, which makes the
'   relationships leaving the cropped area look broken. Read the diagram as SVG, which
'   is also what the documentation build generates, or raise PLANTUML_LIMIT_SIZE.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

namespace score::mw::com {

    ' =======================================================================
    ' Runtime
    ' =======================================================================
    namespace mw_com::binding_independent::runtime <<unit>> {

        class "runtime" as mw_com_binding_independent_runtime_runtime <<free_functions>> {
            '{static} +InitializeRuntimeFromCommandLine(in command_line_arguments : CommandLineArguments)
            {static} +InitializeRuntimeFromCommandLine(command_line_arguments : const CommandLineArguments&)
            '{static} +InitializeRuntimeFromConfiguration(in runtime_configuration : RuntimeConfiguration)
            {static} +InitializeRuntimeFromConfiguration(runtime_configuration : const RuntimeConfiguration&)
            '{static} +AddConfiguration(in runtime_configuration : RuntimeConfiguration) : Result
            {static} +AddConfiguration(runtime_configuration : const RuntimeConfiguration&) : Result
            '{static} +AddConfigurationFromJson(in json : Json) : Result
            {static} +AddConfigurationFromJson(json : Json) : Result
            '{static} +ResolveInstanceIDs(in model_name : InstanceSpecifier) : Result<InstanceIdentifierContainer>
            {static} +ResolveInstanceIDs(model_name : const InstanceSpecifier&) : Result<InstanceIdentifierContainer>
        }

        class "RuntimeConfiguration" as mw_com_binding_independent_runtime_RuntimeConfiguration {
            +ConstructDefault()
            '+CreateFromCommandLine(in command_line_arguments : CommandLineArguments) : RuntimeConfiguration
            +CreateFromCommandLine(command_line_arguments : CommandLineArguments) : RuntimeConfiguration
            '+CreateFromPath(in configuration_path : Path) : RuntimeConfiguration
            +CreateFromPath(configuration_path : Path) : RuntimeConfiguration
            '+GetConfigurationPath() : Path {query}
            +GetConfigurationPath() const : const Path&
        }

        class "CommandLineArguments" as mw_com_binding_independent_runtime_CommandLineArguments <<datatype>>

        note bottom of mw_com_binding_independent_runtime_CommandLineArguments
          No type of the public C++ API, but the abstraction of the command line
          arguments a process was started with, passed either as (argc, argv) or as
          a span of null terminated strings.
        end note

        ' --- relationships inside runtime ---
        mw_com_binding_independent_runtime_runtime ..> mw_com_binding_independent_runtime_RuntimeConfiguration : <<use>>
        mw_com_binding_independent_runtime_runtime ..> mw_com_binding_independent_runtime_CommandLineArguments : <<use>>
        mw_com_binding_independent_runtime_RuntimeConfiguration ..> mw_com_binding_independent_runtime_CommandLineArguments : <<use>>
    }

